data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\"}]},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\"