data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin("},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin("}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is different"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is different"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n-"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n-"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\n- $\\fr"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\\n- $\\\\fr"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\n- $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\\n- $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\n- $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ and $\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}$ are gen"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\\n- $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ and $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}$ are gen"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01GfVjNgUu9NYsb9FzoecKUp","name":"probe","arguments":{"question":"A common \"proof\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}, \\quad\\text{where } \\Delta u = g(x+\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\nthen takes $\\Delta x \\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?","options":[{"label":"$\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ may be undefined","value":"a"},{"label":"$\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0$, so $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$","value":"b"},{"label":"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist","value":"c"},{"label":"$\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The algebra is fine whenever $\\Delta u \\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\n\nWhy the other options are wrong:\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\Delta u \\to 0$ is guaranteed.\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\n- $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta u}$ and $\\frac{\\Delta u}{\\Delta x}$ are genuine ratios of finite numbers, so split"},"partialJson":"{\"question\": \"A common \\\"proof\\\" of the chain rule for $y = f(g(x))$ goes:\\n$$\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x} = \\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}, \\\\quad\\\\text{where } \\\\Delta u = g(x+\\\\Delta x) - g(x),$$\\nthen takes $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$ on both sides. Textbooks call this argument incomplete. What is the gap?\", \"options\": [{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ can equal $0$ for arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$, so the factor $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ may be undefined\",\"value\":\"a\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\Delta u$ might not tend to $0$ as $\\\\Delta x \\\\to 0$, so $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ need not approach $f'(u)$\",\"value\":\"b\"},{\"label\":\"The limit of a product need not equal the product of the limits, even when both limits exist\",\"value\":\"c\"},{\"label\":\"$\\\\frac{dy}{du}$ is not a fraction, so splitting $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta x}$ into two ratios is not allowed\",\"value\":\"d\"}], \"explanation\": \"The algebra is fine whenever $\\\\Delta u \\\\neq 0$. The trouble is that $g$ can take the same value at $x$ and at points arbitrarily close to $x$. For example, $g(x) = x^2\\\\sin(1/x)$ near $0$ hits zero infinitely often, so $\\\\Delta u = 0$ for some arbitrarily small $\\\\Delta x$. At those points you'd be dividing by zero.\\n\\nWhy the other options are wrong:\\n- Since $g$ is differentiable, it is continuous, so $\\\\Delta u \\\\to 0$ is guaranteed.\\n- If both limits exist, the limit of the product does equal the product of the limits.\\n- $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta y}{\\\\Delta u}$ and $\\\\frac{\\\\Delta u}{\\\\Delta x}$ are genuine ratios of finite numbers, so split"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":9460,"cacheWrite":5667,"totalTokens":15135,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":5667},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860732109,"responseId":"msg_011CgMQg5hTUgHaUqjE7F1wU"}}

